    title: 'What Is a Phase-Shifting Transformer (PST)?',
    question: 'What is a phase-shifting transformer (PST) and what does it do?',
    quick: 'A phase-shifting transformer (PST), also called a quadrature booster, is a series unit that controls power flow by injecting a phase-shifted voltage. It changes the phase angle across the line, thereby redirecting real-power flow from overloaded lines to under-loaded parallel paths in a meshed network.',
    sections: [
      ['What it controls', 'In an AC network, real power flows largely according to the phase-angle difference between two points. The PST adds a controllable phase shift, letting the operator adjust how much power flows on a given line.'],
      ['Construction', 'A PST uses a series winding (in the line) and an excitation winding (fed from the bus), with an on-load tap-changer controlling the injected voltage and hence the phase angle.'],
      ['Why it matters', 'PSTs relieve congestion, balance loaded and lightly loaded parallel lines, increase transfer capacity and prevent overloads — increasingly important in interconnected networks and markets.'],
    ],
