    slug: 'transformer-furan-analysis', topic: 'Diagnostics', level: 'Intermediate', minutes: 6,
    title: 'What Is Furan Analysis?',
    question: 'What is furan analysis and what does it tell you?',
    quick: 'Furan analysis measures the furanic compounds in the transformer oil that are released as the paper (cellulose) insulation degrades. The furan content is a direct indicator of the state of aging of the solid insulation, so it complements DGA — which looks at the oil — by telling you how much of the paper has degraded. Rising furans indicate accelerated paper aging or a local hot spot.',
    sections: [
      ['What it measures', 'Furans are the by-products of cellulose (paper) degradation. The most common, 2FAL (2-furfuraldehyde), is measured in the oil and its level indicates the degree of paper aging.'],
      ['Why it is useful', 'DGA sees the gas in the oil, but furans specifically show the condition of the paper insulation — the aging/damage that is the real life-limiting factor.'],
      ['Interpreting', 'A rising furan level (or a sudden jump) indicates accelerated aging or a hot spot; combined with DGA, oil tests and a DP sample, it helps you decide the remaining life and the next action.'],
    ],
    refs: ['IEC 60599', 'IEEE C57.104'],
