    title: 'What Is the Paper-Oil Insulation System?',
    question: 'What is the paper-oil insulation system?',
    quick: 'Most large oil-filled transformers use a combined paper-oil insulation system: the solid cellulose (kraft paper, pressboard) provides the mechanical and dielectric structure, and the insulating oil fills the interstices, cools and adds dielectric strength. The oil and paper work together, so the condition of both — moisture, aging, contamination — determines the insulation strength and the transformer life.',
    sections: [
      ['The roles', 'The paper and pressboard give the mechanical support and the main dielectric barrier; the oil fills the gaps, cools the windings and adds dielectric strength (both are better together than apart).'],
      ['Why they work together', 'The oil impregnaates the paper, eliminating air gaps that would otherwise cause partial discharge. The paper is strong and the oil provides the resistance to breakdown in the gaps.'],
      ['Why condition matters', 'Moisture and aging degrade both: water in the paper lowers the dielectric strength, and the paper aging releases water, acids and gases. Keeping the oil dry and the paper cool is the key to life.'],
    ],
    refs: ['IEC 60076-1', 'IEC 60599'],
