    title: 'What Is the Difference Between a Reactor and a Transformer?',
    question: 'What is the difference between a reactor and a transformer?',
    quick: 'A transformer transfers power between two circuits with a defined voltage ratio; a reactor (shunt or series) is an inductive device that absorbs or regulates reactive power with no secondary (or a fixed coupling) — it is essentially an inductor, often a single-winding device. A shunt reactor absorbs reactive power to control voltage; a series reactor limits current/rating. Transformers step voltage; reactors absorb reactance.',
    sections: [
      ['Transformer', 'A two-winding (or autotransformer) device that transfers power with a voltage ratio, isolating or stepping the voltage, with losses, tappings and a magnetic coupling.'],
      ['Reactor', 'A mainly inductive device — a shunt reactor absorbs reactive power to hold the voltage, a series reactor limits current and fault level; it has a low magnetic coupling and no true secondary.'],
      ['How to tell', 'A transformer steps the voltage and has a rating in MVA and a ratio; a reactor is rated in MVAr (shunt) or ohms/current (series) and its main effect is on reactive power or fault current, not voltage transformation.'],
    ],
