    question: 'What causes a winding hot spot (overheating)?',
    quick: 'A winding hot spot is a local over-temperature caused by uneven cooling or current distribution, a partial oil-flow block, a stray loss, a poor connection, or an overload. The winding-temperature gradient and the design set the hottest-spot. A persistent hot spot ages the insulation locally and can cause an overheating or thermal failure. It is seen by temperature monitoring, DGA and a thermal inspection.',
