    quick: 'A shunt reactor is connected phase-to-earth (shunt) to absorb reactive power and control the voltage; a series reactor is connected in series with the line to limit the current and the fault level. The shunt reactor is rated in MVAr and reduces the voltage, while the series reactor is rated in ohms/current and limits the short-circuit and inrush current. They serve different purposes but are both single-winding inductive devices.',
